WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:06.920
One of our oldest and most popular videos,
just recently celebrated its fourth anniversary.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.704
So we made this shirt to celebrate!

00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:13.960
Because you may look at a tardigrade and you
might not assume that the two of you have

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.690
a lot in common, but like the resilient water
bear, I think we all have those days where

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.480
we feel a little chubby, misunderstood, and
not immortal.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.756
So if you'd like to pick one up for yourself,
you can get them right now at microcosmos.store.

00:00:52.516 --> 00:00:56.533
When was the last time you saw a puddle?

00:00:58.462 --> 00:00:59.649
Was it recent—

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:04.423
perhaps some time in the past week, fresh
from a downpour?

00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:09.650
Or has it been a long time since you’ve
seen rain, and so an even longer time since

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.323
your path has crossed a puddle?

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.439
If you can remember, do you know what you
did when you saw the puddle?

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:22.202
Did you hop around it, trying to keep your
clothes as pristine as possible?

00:01:22.202 --> 00:01:27.081
Or did you have the complete opposite impulse
and jump right in the middle of it,

00:01:27.081 --> 00:01:30.172
and splashing water everywhere.

00:01:30.172 --> 00:01:36.899
Or maybe you leaned in closer and wondered 
what’s in there, floating a few inches away in the

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:39.720
shallow depths at the edge of your feet?

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.619
If that last one sounds like something you
did (or maybe something you wish you had done),

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:46.440
you’re in good company.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.216
Everything we’re watching right now came
from puddles around Warsaw that James, our

00:01:51.216 --> 00:01:54.390
master of microscopes, collected samples from.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.970
He waited for two days after it rained and
then wandered from puddle to puddle so he

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.923
could collect water to watch under the microscope.

00:02:02.923 --> 00:02:06.704
That’s where he found these green algae
called pandorina.

00:02:06.704 --> 00:02:13.200
This is not actually one algae, but rather
a group of them, bound together in a mucus-y

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.500
shell so they can form a colony.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:21.180
You can see those individual members of the
colony better here, each a single green cell

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.183
living a conjoined existence with its neighbors.

00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:29.320
Given enough light, these colonies quickly
reproduce, crowding the tiny puddles that

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.621
James gathers his samples from.

00:02:31.621 --> 00:02:36.200
But James is not the first microscopist to
peer into a puddle.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.440
In fact, if we look at the writings of one
of the original masters of microscopes, we

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:48.948
can transport ourselves in time and space
to the microcosmos of a 17th century Dutch puddle.

00:02:48.948 --> 00:02:54.810
I’m of course talking about Antony van Leeuwenhoek,
a Dutch microscopist whose many letters to

00:02:54.810 --> 00:03:00.260
the Royal Society of London contain our earliest
descriptions of microorganisms, or as he called

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.563
them “little animalcules”.

00:03:04.424 --> 00:03:09.904
When Leeuwenhoek first mentioned these little
animalcules, not everyone believed him.

00:03:09.904 --> 00:03:13.760
It was one thing to hear his description of
a bee stinger under the microscope.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.091
People were familiar with bees and their stingers.

00:03:17.091 --> 00:03:19.950
But an invisible world of tiny creatures—

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:25.811
tinier, according to Leeuwenhoek, than the
mites found on a cheese rind, well, that was

00:03:25.811 --> 00:03:27.459
harder to imagine.

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:31.335
In a letter to the microscopist Robert Hooke,
Leeuwenhoek wrote,

00:03:31.335 --> 00:03:36.560
“I suffer many contractions and oft-times
hear it said that I do but tell fairy tales

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.074
about the little animals.”

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:41.775
One of Leeuwenhoek’s letters started with
the following:

00:03:41.775 --> 00:03:47.730
In 1675 I discovered living creatures in Rain
water which had stood but few days in a new

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:48.881
earthen pot.

00:03:51.642 --> 00:03:56.379
This rainwater, along with the sampling methods,
observations, and experiments that Leeuwenhoek

00:03:56.379 --> 00:04:01.240
described in his letters, would form the basis
of his own understanding of the microscopic world.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:06.689
And so, in turn, it would form the basis of
many other’s investigations into the little

00:04:06.689 --> 00:04:10.151
animals and the world they inhabit all around
us.

00:04:10.151 --> 00:04:15.500
In a way then, every time you see a puddle,
you can think of it as a natural ode to the

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.870
early experiments that introduced us to the
microcosmos—

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.375
like little memorials to the history of microscopy.

00:04:22.375 --> 00:04:25.870
Puddles are more than just reminders of history
though.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:31.385
They are their own little worlds, placing
demands on the organisms living inside of them.

00:04:31.385 --> 00:04:37.380
This cyst, for example, holds a rotifer that
is slowly waking up from a nap that had probably

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.242
protected it from a dry spell, before rain
had filled the puddle back up.

00:04:41.242 --> 00:04:46.120
There is a rhythm to that kind of life, tied
to the wetness surrounding them.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.632
In between rains, the organisms must find
a way to cope with their dry environment.

00:04:50.632 --> 00:04:56.560
And when the world around them becomes wet
again, the organisms thrive, like this ciliate

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.535
that’s dividing so it can fill its watery
world up with clones.

00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:06.590
These are boom and bust cycles taken to their
extreme, to a million little worlds that go

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.545
from not existing to existing when you just
add water.

00:05:11.545 --> 00:05:17.580
So it’s no wonder microscopists have been
drawn to puddles for centuries, these little

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:22.422
pop-up microcosmos-es that reveal pockets
of life lying all around us.

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:26.990
This video of a vorticella comes to us from
a master microscopist in Florida named Curtis,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.260
who was sampling driveway puddles to watch
under the Journey to the Microcosmos microscope.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.740
We can see it in the middle of our screen,
perfectly still with its stalk extended long

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:43.490
until something comes along and spooks it,
sending the vorticella rapidly inward as it

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.522
contracts back towards the debris it’s attached
to.

00:05:46.522 --> 00:05:52.660
This footage was submitted to us over at microcosmos.store,
where, if you’re interested, you can submit

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:57.949
your own footage to potentially be used in
a Microcosmos episode, because there are countless

00:05:57.949 --> 00:06:02.970
worlds all around us waiting to be seen and
shared, and we’re looking forward to seeing

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.477
more of the worlds around you 
fellow masters of microscopes.

00:06:07.477 --> 00:06:11.919
And after everything we’ve learned today,
it feels right that the first video we would

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:16.260
be able to share from one of you comes from
a puddle, that it comes from a source that

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.880
shares so much with the home of Leeunwehoek’s
little animalcules despite originating centuries

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.058
later and thousands of miles away.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.509
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:38.400
Don't forget right now you can head on over
to microcosmos.store both to submit some footage

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.810
if you like, but also to pick up our brand
new Tardigrade T-shirt celebrating the fourth

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.015
anniversary of our very first Tardigrade video.

00:06:47.014 --> 00:06:51.651
Before we go, we also want to thank each and
every single one of our Patreon patrons, because

00:06:51.651 --> 00:06:54.999
without them, this show could not exist.

00:06:54.999 --> 00:06:57.229
And you're seeing some of their names on the
screen right now.

00:06:57.229 --> 00:07:02.706
And if you would like to join those names
on the screen, you can go to Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro.

00:07:02.706 --> 00:07:05.750
If you'd like to see more from our Master
of Microscopes, James Weiss, you can check

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.901
out Jam and Jerms on Instagram.

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:12.434
And if you'd like to see more from us, there's
probably a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

